# A MICROVM WITH A SCREEN, A ROOT FILESYSTEM, AND A RECORDING THAT SHOWS BOTH.
#
# THE EXISTING `verify.walk` PROVES THE VOCABULARY RUNS A GUEST, and it can only look at the SERIAL
# LINE: its guest is Alpine's diskless netboot, which has no root filesystem of its own and draws
# nothing on the VGA adapter. MEASURED: a screendump of that guest is 1093 bytes of black with one row
# of boot banner, for the whole run.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE OTHER SHAPE. It boots a root filesystem the CI builds (see `alpine-rootfs.sh`), so:
#
#   - the kernel has somewhere to switch_root to, and `/sbin/init` runs
#   - `tty1` gets a getty, so the guest DRAWS ITS LOGIN PROMPT ON THE VGA ADAPTER
#   - `graphics=on` (the default) means the recorder has something to photograph
#
# THE THREE THINGS THAT MAKE IT WORK, AND EACH WAS MEASURED THE HARD WAY:
#
# 1. `kernel_append` IS REQUIRED. The engine's default is Alpine's diskless line -
#    `console=ttyS0 modules=loop,squashfs,sd-mod modloop=/dev/vda` - and a guest whose root is an
#    EXT4 disk on /dev/vda mounts it, then has `modloop=/dev/vda` mounted OVER it, then drops to
#    `initramfs emergency recovery shell launched`. Without this key the walk sits in
#    "the console will say login" until its timeout, which is exactly what it did.
#
# 2. THE INITRAMFS MUST CARRY ext4. The netboot initramfs has 72 modules and ext4 is not one of them,
#    so `mount /dev/vda` answers `No such device`. `mkinitfs` with `ext4 virtio_blk` is what fixes it.
#
# 3. `console=tty0` AS WELL AS `ttyS0`, so the drawn output reaches the screen the recorder reads.
#    With only `ttyS0` the guest is perfectly healthy and the video is black.

walkthrough "alpine microvm - a desktop on the VGA adapter, and a recording of it"
resolution 1024x768
# THE VIDEO IS THE ARTIFACT, so its size is stated rather than inherited.
recording_resolution 1024x768


step "needs alpine-vmlinuz alpine-initramfs alpine.qcow2"
  needs [alpine-vmlinuz, alpine-initramfs, alpine.qcow2]

  # THE ROOT FILESYSTEM, BUILT BY THE CI STEP AND NOT INSTALLED BY THIS FILE.
  # A microvm has no firmware and no installer: the disk arrives ready, which is what makes the run
  # ninety seconds rather than forty minutes.

step "disk alpine.qcow2"
  disk alpine.qcow2


step "boot as a microvm from kernel \"alpine-vmlinuz\" and initramfs \"alpine-initramfs\" with \"console=tty0 console=ttyS0 root=/dev/vda rw rootfstype=ext4\", waiting up to 180s"
  boot
  initrd alpine-initramfs
  kernel alpine-vmlinuz
  kernel_append "console=tty0 console=ttyS0 root=/dev/vda rw rootfstype=ext4"
  microvm
  os microvm
  timeout 180

  # THE GUEST SAYS THIS ON ITS OWN SERIAL LINE, and it is the fact the boot worked: init finished and
  # getty is asking for a login.

step "the console will say \"login\""
  until login
  from console
  timeout 180

  # AND THIS IS THE PART THE OLD FILE COULD NOT DO. The recording runs for the whole walk, so these
  # twenty seconds are twenty frames of a login prompt drawn on the VGA adapter - which is what makes
  # the mp4 a picture of the guest rather than a black field.

step "wait 20s"
  settle 20


step "stop the machine by killing it, waiting up to 60s"
  stop kill
  timeout 60
